technology.technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesse:
The pulp, or fibres from the wood are separated in a kraft cooking process in a so-called fibre line. In a recovery line, the chemicals used in the cooking process are recovered to be used again and energy is produced. The third process is effluent treatment where gaseous and liquid discharges to the environment are continuously minimized and monitored. The process includes transports to the pulp mill, wood handling, chemical pulping, drying process, energy production on-site, recovery cycles of chemicals and internal waste water treatment. The following air emissions were reported in the study: CO2 (fossil and biogenic), CO, SOx (as SO2), NOx, TRS (as S), SO2 (reported as S), Dust (unspecified). Next to these key emissions, additional emissions from on-site energy production from external and internal fuels were calculated, using emission profiles from ecoinvent combustion processes. Internal biogenic fuels are represented by the calculated emissions but do not have the corresponding fuel input from technosphere, because they are supplied from within the system boundaries. The amounts of internal fuels used in the process are: Biogas (6.34E+01 MJ/t), Bark (1.34E+02MJ/t), Black liquor(1.98E+04MJ/t), Sludge (3.64E+00MJ/t), Methanol (1.73E+02MJ/t), Biomass (1.12E+02 MJ/t), tall oil pitch (4.14E+02 MJ/t)
The following ecoinvent combustion processes were used to estimate additional air emissions: Biofuel - Biogas: heat production, natural gas, at industrial furnace >100kW, Europe without Switzerland; Peat: heat production, lignite briquette, at stove 5-15kW; Hard coal: heat production, at hard coal industrial furnace 1-10MW, Europe without Switzerland; Heavy fuel oil: heat production, heavy fuel oil, at industrial furnace 1MW, Europe without Switzerland; Light fuel oil: heat production, light fuel oil, at industrial furnace 1MW, Europe without Switzerland; Methanol: heat production, natural gas, at industrial furnace >100kW, Europe without Switzerland; Natural gas: heat production, natural gas, at industrial furnace >100kW, Europe without Switzerland; Biofuel - Bark : heat production, wood chips from industry, at furnace 1000kW, CH; Biofuel - Biomass: heat production, wood chips from industry, at furnace 1000kW, CH;
Black liquor, sludge, tall oil pitch, tar oil and sodium formiate were not included in the emissions calculations because no specific emission profiles, e.g. for the black liquor recovery process, were available. Emissions from these internal energy generation processes are therefore only reflected in the key emission factors provided by the industry, other emissions constitute a data gap. Emissions of stemming from biogas are approximated with the combustion of natural gas, whereby CO2, CO and CH4 emissions stemming from biogas were modelled as non-fossil. Tar oil which is an external fuel (4.44E+01 MJ/t) has also no corresponding fuel input from technosphere because no appropriate proxy could be found. The amount of infrastructure (pulp factory) was taken from the dataset “The European Pulp Industry Sector Association AISBL, sulfate pulp production, from hardwood, bleached, RER, Undefined, ecoinvent database version 3.7.1.”
